libmdr Python Bindings
===

Installation
---
pip install libmdr        # core bindings (mdr-shared)
pip install libmdr[bt]    # + libmdr-bt, the platform Bluetooth backend
pip install libmdr[cli]   # + libmdr-cli, the `mdr` command line tool. see `mdr help`

The extras are shorthands for the standalone packages, i.e. `pip install libmdr-bt`
and `pip install libmdr-cli` work just as well.

Prebuilt wheels work with any Python >= 3.10 (CPython, free-threaded CPython or PyPy) on:
- Windows   x86-64, arm64
- macOS 12+ x86-64, arm64
- Linux     x86-64, arm64 (glibc >= 2.28); armv7 (glibc >= 2.31)

On Linux, libmdr-bt uses the system's libdbus-1 and libbluetooth (BlueZ).
Elsewhere pip builds from the sdist, which needs CMake >= 3.31, git and a C++20 compiler.

Development
---
pip install -e libmdr
pip install -e libmdr-bt
pip install -e libmdr-cli # optional. the `mdr` command line tool, see `mdr help`

Releasing
---
CI (.github/workflows/wheels.yml) builds the wheels and sdists on every push.
Pushing a `vX.Y.Z` tag that matches the version of all three pyproject.toml files
also publishes them to PyPI.

Regenerating the C ABI Bindings
---
With SonyHeadphonesClient checked out next to this repository:

python tooling/generate_bindings.py [--source path-to-SonyHeadphonesClient]
